Restore the audio.cpp fork's contiguous concat fast paths on the CPU backend
(the fork tags these sites MINITTS_CONCAT_FASTPATH).

REBASED for ggml 0.22.0 (36da5713). Two things changed upstream and this patch
had to be reworked rather than merely re-offset:

  1. ggml_compute_forward_concat_any was REWRITTEN. In 0.20.2 it walked every
     element scalar-wise ("TODO: smarter multi-theading"), which is what this
     delta originally restored. 0.22.0 now does row-wise memcpy itself, adds
     GGML_ASSERT(ggml_is_contiguous_rows(...)), and became block-size aware.
     Upstream has therefore CONVERGED on most of this fork delta. What remains
     is the memcpy COUNT for the fully-contiguous dim-1 case: ne01 + ne11 row
     copies per plane collapse to 2 plane copies. That is still worth keeping
     for the per-head attention concat loop, but the margin is far smaller than
     it was against 0.20.2 -- a benchmark should decide whether to retire this
     half of the patch at the next bump.
  2. The `len` local (ggml_type_size(src0->type)) that this patch's byte math
     used no longer exists in concat_any. Byte counts now go through
     ggml_row_size, which is block-aware; the old bare ggml_type_size product
     would have over-counted for quantized types, so the rebase also removes a
     latent (in practice unreached) defect in our own delta. Type equality
     between src0/src1/dst is now asserted explicitly.

ggml_compute_forward_concat_f32 is UNCHANGED upstream -- it still dispatches
scalar element-wise -- so its dim-0 row and dim-1 plane fast paths carry their
full original win and are reproduced verbatim.

The framework concatenates per-head attention outputs in a loop (the
ExplicitCpuPerHead lowering in
src/framework/modules/attention/scaled_dot_product_attention.cpp), so this is a
hot path, and losing it in the 0.20.2 convergence was a silent perf regression
rather than a correctness one.

Behaviour is unchanged: every fast path is gated on full contiguity (and, for
concat_any, matching types) and falls through to the upstream loop otherwise.

